  "narrative": "<h3 class=\"story-subheading\">Short impact summary</h3>\n<p class=\"story-text\">This preprint is already drawing early scholarly attention: Scite records 16 citations, with one citing statement categorized as supporting and the remainder as neutral mentions. The broader record is still sparse, but the paper has also surfaced in a small amount of public discussion, including one Substack mention, suggesting that its claims about how LLMs can shift both tone and meaning are beginning to circulate beyond the immediate research context.</p>\n<h3 class=\"story-subheading\">Full narrative</h3>\n<p class=\"story-text\">\u201cHow LLMs Distort Our Written Language\u201d argues that large language models do more than polish prose: they can change the voice, tone, and intended meaning of human writing. The abstract points to a user study showing that extensive LLM use was associated with a substantial rise in essays that stayed neutral when responding to a topic prompt, underscoring the paper\u2019s central concern that model-assisted writing may subtly flatten expression as well as content.</p>\n<p class=\"story-text\">The early impact record is modest but real. Scite shows 16 citing statements overall, but only one is classed as supporting, with the rest appearing as neutral mentions rather than direct validation or dispute. That pattern is consistent with a very new preprint: the work is being noticed, but the literature has not yet had time to build a dense citation trail around it.</p>\n<p class=\"story-text\">Public attention remains limited. The digest reports one web mention, on Substack, which indicates that the paper has entered at least some broader conversation about generative AI and writing. No stronger media signal is present in the record provided, so the available evidence points to a nascent but visible footprint rather than wide public uptake.</p>\n<p class=\"story-text\">Other signals are similarly early-stage. OpenAIRE records the work as openly accessible and indexed across multiple repository instances, which should help discovery and reuse. The record also shows zero counted views and downloads in the available DataCite feed, so the clearest story here is not reach at scale, but a recently posted study whose claims have begun to attract scholarly and commentary attention while its long-term influence is still unfolding.</p>",
